WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.190
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:06.150
is refresh our understanding
of place value but we're

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.960
going to dig a little bit
deeper and think about place

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.860
value in the context of decimals.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.537
So just as a refresher
if I have the number 973,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.670
this should be review for you.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:22.631
We already know that this
rightmost space right over here,

00:00:22.631 --> 00:00:27.631
this is the ones place and if
we move one space to the left

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.230
of that, this is the tens place.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.700
Notice we went from ones
to tens, tens are ten times

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:35.840
as much as ones.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.020
And then we move one
space to the left of that,

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.020
we go to the we multiply by tens again.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.840
We get to the hundreds space
and so this nine doesn't just

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.800
represent nine, it represents
nine hundred or we could write

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.580
that as 900.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.920
Similarly the seven doesn't
just represent seven,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.600
it represents seven tens or 70.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.850
This three represents three
ones, so it actually does

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.670
represent three.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.110
But as I promised we're
now going to extend our

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.570
understanding and what we
do is we put a decimal here

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.210
which you've probably
seen before at the right

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.822
and the reason why we even
need a decimal is to really

00:01:14.822 --> 00:01:17.710
tell us where our ones place is.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.790
We say okay if we go right
to the left of the decimal

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.130
that's going to be our one
space because once we start

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.920
introducing decimals we can
introduce as many spaces

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.730
as we want to the right of the decimal.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.000
And so let's think about
those a little bit.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.410
If when we went from hundreds
to tens, notice we divided

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.208
by ten, when we go from tens
to ones, notice you divide

00:01:37.208 --> 00:01:38.410
by ten.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.550
So what do you think this
place over here is going to be

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:41.640
called?

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.599
Well what happens if you
take one divided by ten?

00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:48.062
Well then you get a tenth
so as you might imagine

00:01:48.062 --> 00:01:50.400
this is the tenths place.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.610
And then if you were to
go one place to the right

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.670
of that, what would this place be?

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:00.109
Well it'd be tenths divided
by ten or 1/10 of a tenth,

00:02:00.109 --> 00:02:05.109
so this would be a
hundredth, hundredths place.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.120
And then if you were to
go one space to the right

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.330
we could keep doing this
forever, but if we were to go

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.023
one space to the right of
that, what would it be?

00:02:14.023 --> 00:02:19.023
Well a hundredth divided by
ten or 1/10 of a hundredth

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.570
is a thousandth, thousandth space.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.320
And so for example if I
were to extend this number

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:32.113
instead of if just being 973,
if I were to write 973.526,

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.610
what do these numbers
these digits represent?

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.060
This five doesn't just
represent five, it represents

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:49.050
five tenths or another
way of writing five tenths

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.960
you could write it like this
0.5 you just have a five

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.080
in the tenths place.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.600
Or you could write it as five tenths.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.490
This two I think you
get where this is going,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.800
this doesn't just represent
two, it represents

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.610
two hundredths I'm just going
to make it very explicit

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.730
in this video, so it's
very clear two hundredths.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.270
Another way to write that
is you just write a two

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.540
in the hundredths place.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.216
So we're going one two spaces
to the right of the decimal

00:03:17.216 --> 00:03:22.022
or you could write it as two
over 100, two hundredths.

00:03:22.022 --> 00:03:25.300
And so for kicks, pause
the video what are all

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.474
the different ways of
representing this six?

00:03:27.474 --> 00:03:29.592
What does this six represent?

00:03:29.592 --> 00:03:34.592
Well this is six thousandths,
six thousandths, thousandths,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.880
there you go.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.980
I could also write that as
zero point, let's see it's

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.910
the tenths place,
hundredths place, and then

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:47.410
in the thousandths place I
have six or I could write

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:52.410
this as six over 1000, six thousandths.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.470
So big picture place value we
can keep going to the right

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.672
of the decimal and we can
start representing things

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:03.463
that are I guess you
could say more precise.

